<div class="container-fluid main-container">




<div id="header">



<h1 class="title toc-ignore">Evaluating land consolidation decisions for
smallholder farmers in Kenya</h1>
<h4 class="author">Hannah Kamau<sup>a,@</sup>, Cory
Whitney<sup>a,b</sup> <br /><small></small><sup>a</sup>Center of Development
Research (ZEF), University of Bonn, Genscherallee 3, 53113 Bonn, Germany
<br /><small><sup>b</sup>INRES-Horticultural Sciences, University of
Bonn, Auf dem Huegel 6, 53121 Bonn, Germany
<br /><small></small><sup>@</sup>Corresponding author’s email:<a href="mailto:hannah.nyakio@gmail.com" class="email">hannah.nyakio@gmail.com</a></h4>

</div>


<div id="introduction" class="section level1">
<h1>Introduction</h1>
<p>This document is a supplementary resource describing the land
consolidation model used to simulate the outcomes of the decision to
consolidate land in Kenya by smallholder farmers. The data are expert
estimates collected in March 2024, after co-creating a conceptual model
with stakeholders, supplemented with published literature where
available and were unavailable other stakeholders not in in the workshop
were contacted to discuss the estimates.</p>
</div>
<div id="required-packages" class="section level1">
<h1>Required packages</h1>
<p>The core R packages are <code>decisionSupport</code> <span class="citation">(<a href="#ref-R-decisionSupport">Luedeling et al.
2025</a>)</span>, <code>tidyverse</code> <span class="citation">(<a href="#ref-R-tidyverse">Wickham 2023</a>)</span>, and
<code>ggplot2</code> <span class="citation">(<a href="#ref-ggplot22016">Wickham 2016</a>)</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="from-conceptual-model-to-a-land-consolidation-function" class="section level1">
<h1>From conceptual model to a land consolidation function</h1>
<p>The conceptual model, co-created with stakeholders, reflects the
costs, benefits, and risks that could arise when shifting from small,
highly diversified farms to a model where farmers consolidate their land
under external management.</p>
<p><strong>Costs a farmer could incur under land
consolidation</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Planning costs (legal fees, knowledge acquisition, stationery,
printing)</li>
<li>Loss of natural assets, e.g. perennial plants such as trees</li>
<li>Food costs no longer saved</li>
<li>Household upkeep cost prior to the first payment</li>
<li>Medical bills due to a reduced active lifestyle</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Benefits a farmer could gain under land
consolidation</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Lease income</li>
<li>Saved production costs</li>
<li>Alternative income from on- or off-farm activities</li>
<li>Better childhood outcomes</li>
<li>Social cohesion</li>
<li>Saved medical bills related to land-use accidents/diseases</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Costs incurred by a farmer who remains in the current system
(“do nothing”)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Planning costs (incurred whether or not the farmer changes)</li>
<li>Medical bills related to land-use accidents/diseases and stress</li>
<li>Production costs</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Benefits under the “do nothing” option</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Land security (valued via trees)</li>
<li>Annual crop yield</li>
<li>Saved food costs</li>
</ul>
<p>Both options face risks: farmers who shift face social risks from
freed-up time, while those who do nothing face crop-related risks.</p>
<p><em>Shifting to land consolidation is modeled over a 25-year period,
during which the farmer can no longer farm the consolidated parcel but
receives lease income commensurate with its size.</em></p>
<p>The <code>land_consolidation_function</code> defines these costs,
benefits, and risks for both options. The <strong>decision</strong> is
the difference between the two options.</p>
</div>
<div id="inputs-model-function-and-test-variables" class="section level1">
<h1>Inputs, model function, and test variables</h1>
<p>The input file which contains the variables and estimates –&gt;
<code>land_consolidation_input.csv</code>. Market scenario file based on
value of information –&gt; <code>market_scenario.csv</code> which is
used later.</p>
<pre class="r"><code>input_path    &lt;- &quot;land_consolidation_input.csv&quot;
base_estimate &lt;- decisionSupport::estimate_read_csv(input_path, sep = &quot;;&quot;)
input_table   &lt;- read.csv(input_path, sep = &quot;;&quot;)</code></pre>
<pre class="r"><code>make_variables &lt;- function(est, n = 1) {
  x &lt;- decisionSupport::random(rho = est, n = n)
  for (i in colnames(x)) assign(i, as.numeric(x[1, i]), envir = .GlobalEnv)
}
make_variables(base_estimate)</code></pre>
<pre class="r"><code>source(&quot;land_consolidation_function.R&quot;)</code></pre>
</div>
<div id="monte-carlo-simulation" class="section level1">
<h1>Monte Carlo simulation</h1>
<p>We run a Monte Carlo simulation of the model function using the
inputs in <code>land_consolidation_input.csv</code>, and in this model
we use 10,000 model runs. Full model described here can be found in the
<code>land_consolidation_function.R</code></p>
<pre class="r"><code>mcSimulation_results &lt;- decisionSupport::mcSimulation(
  estimate          = base_estimate,
  model_function    = land_consolidation_function,
  numberOfModelRuns = 1e2,
  functionSyntax    = &quot;plainNames&quot;)</code></pre>
</div>
<div id="results" class="section level1">
<h1>Results</h1>
<div id="distribution-of-the-outcomes" class="section level2">
<h2>Distribution of the outcomes</h2>
<p>We present 2 key results:- (i) The distribution of the 2 decision
options (i) land consolidation and (ii) maintain farms; and (ii) the
distribution of decision do which is reflected by the difference of the
options.</p>
<pre class="r"><code># Assemble model inputs (x) and outputs (y), then express NPVs per hectare.
mc_output &lt;- cbind(as.data.frame(mcSimulation_results$y),
                   as.data.frame(mcSimulation_results$x)) %&gt;%
  mutate(NPV_per_ha          = benefit_lc_kes        / ha_per_hh,
         No_NPV_per_ha       = benefit_status_quo_kes / ha_per_hh,
         NPV_per_ha_decision = net_benefit_lc_kes     / ha_per_hh)

# Summary statistics (million KES).
summarised_stats &lt;- mc_output %&gt;%
  select(NPV_per_ha, No_NPV_per_ha, NPV_per_ha_decision) %&gt;%
  summarise(across(everything(), list(
    min         = ~min(., na.rm = TRUE) / 1e6,
    q05         = ~quantile(., 0.05, na.rm = TRUE) / 1e6,
    median      = ~median(., na.rm = TRUE) / 1e6,
    q95         = ~quantile(., 0.95, na.rm = TRUE) / 1e6,
    max         = ~max(., na.rm = TRUE) / 1e6,
    pc_positive = ~mean(. &gt; 0, na.rm = TRUE) * 100))) %&gt;%
  pivot_longer(everything(),
               names_to = c(&quot;variable&quot;, &quot;statistic&quot;),
               names_pattern = &quot;^(.*)_(.*)$&quot;, values_to = &quot;value&quot;) %&gt;%
  pivot_wider(names_from = statistic, values_from = value) %&gt;%
  mutate(across(min:max, \(x) round(x, 2)))
summarised_stats</code></pre>
<pre><code>## # A tibble: 6 × 7
##   variable                    min    q05 median   q95   max positive
##   &lt;chr&gt;                     &lt;dbl&gt;  &lt;dbl&gt;  &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt;
